WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.601
January 2021,

00:00:04.861 --> 00:00:05.201
Dover.

00:00:06.381 --> 00:00:09.682
Customs controls on the border between the UK and the EU.

00:00:10.442 --> 00:00:12.663
For the first time in 28 years.

00:00:14.042 --> 00:00:16.984
Although a last-minute trade deal has avoided total chaos,

00:00:17.364 --> 00:00:18.724
this is a hard Brexit.

00:00:19.405 --> 00:00:24.246
Much harder than the Brexit that was promised to the British people back in 2016.

00:00:26.766 --> 00:00:27.746
During the referendum,

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:28.467
they all said,

00:00:28.487 --> 00:00:28.606
no,

00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:28.766
no,

00:00:28.787 --> 00:00:28.907
no,

00:00:28.908 --> 00:00:29.027
no,

00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:29.187
no,

00:00:29.347 --> 00:00:30.968
it's not a hard Brexit what we want.

00:00:34.055 --> 00:00:38.699
The experts agree that this Brexit will cause economic damage to both sides.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.044
Even the government in London expects a major slump in the British economy.

00:00:45.726 --> 00:00:46.766
How did it come to this?

00:00:47.887 --> 00:00:49.829
It's becoming more and more clear.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.352
Hard Brexit was a well-planned coup.

00:00:57.323 --> 00:01:01.968
The concept of Brexit that was about let's get back control of our borders,

00:01:02.027 --> 00:01:03.668
take back control of our fishing,

00:01:03.689 --> 00:01:04.969
they kind of got hijacked.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.632
But who did want a hard Brexit?

00:01:09.513 --> 00:01:11.394
And who stands to profit from it?

00:01:13.663 --> 00:01:15.944
There's a clear ideological driving force here,

00:01:15.984 --> 00:01:19.465
which is around a desire for a very low-tax,

00:01:19.805 --> 00:01:20.986
low-regulation economy.

00:01:21.206 --> 00:01:21.326
Now,

00:01:21.327 --> 00:01:25.167
the problem is that it's not what a lot of people who voted for Brexit want.

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:34.731
This film tells the story of how a small group from the heart of the British establishment hijacked the Brexit course to pursue their own radical agenda.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.439
We start our journey in Glasgow.

00:01:46.281 --> 00:01:53.012
The investigative journalist Peter Gagan has spent years looking into how money influences British politics.

00:01:53.532 --> 00:01:55.496
With a particular focus on Brexit.

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:04.864
What we saw in the Brexit referendum on its aftermath is that abuses of electoral law,

00:02:05.304 --> 00:02:06.784
small amounts of targeted money,

00:02:06.924 --> 00:02:08.667
anonymous donations can really,

00:02:08.806 --> 00:02:11.168
really change the political landscape in really,

00:02:11.268 --> 00:02:12.288
really radical ways.

00:02:12.748 --> 00:02:13.870
And how exactly does that work?

00:02:13.889 --> 00:02:14.489
work?

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:19.073
So Britain is really like America when it comes to politics and money but it's a big difference.

00:02:19.074 --> 00:02:22.976
In America it costs millions and millions and millions of dollars to influence politics.

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:25.338
In Britain it costs only a fraction of that.

00:02:25.598 --> 00:02:27.079
A few thousand pounds here,

00:02:27.239 --> 00:02:32.163
a few tens of thousand pounds here have a far bigger impact than I think many people will be able to imagine.

00:02:33.868 --> 00:02:35.148
Back in 2016,

00:02:35.469 --> 00:02:37.769
the leading figures of the Brexit campaign,

00:02:38.088 --> 00:02:40.030
Boris Johnson and Nigel Farage,

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.651
won the referendum with big promises for ordinary people.

00:02:47.672 --> 00:02:49.513
If we vote Leave on June 23,

00:02:49.514 --> 00:02:52.194
we can take back control of 350...

00:02:52.195 --> 00:02:53.734
More money for the NHS.

00:02:54.094 --> 00:02:56.155
..and spend on our national health service.

00:02:57.235 --> 00:02:58.216
Less immigration.

00:03:00.423 --> 00:03:01.525
And more fish.

00:03:02.185 --> 00:03:04.987
The fish stocks are in our territorial waters,

00:03:05.127 --> 00:03:06.308
and if we vote for Brexit,

00:03:06.709 --> 00:03:07.789
we'll save this industry.

00:03:08.631 --> 00:03:10.471
But despite the populist slogans,

00:03:10.792 --> 00:03:19.218
investigative research has since shown that the leave campaigns were financed by over 18.3 million pounds from rich donors.

00:03:24.216 --> 00:03:27.000
The UK has voted to leave the European Union.

00:03:27.581 --> 00:03:29.644
The result of the referendum was tight.

00:03:30.044 --> 00:03:30.645
52%

00:03:31.186 --> 00:03:33.069
voted for a break with the EU.

00:03:33.349 --> 00:03:36.593
The sun has risen on an independent,

00:03:36.794 --> 00:03:37.814
united kingdom.

00:03:38.436 --> 00:03:39.157
And just look at it,

00:03:39.356 --> 00:03:40.558
even the weather's improved.

00:03:41.548 --> 00:03:43.868
But for many of the rich supporters of Brexit,

00:03:44.149 --> 00:03:46.550
the vote was not the end of their campaign,

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:47.611
but the beginning.

00:03:48.151 --> 00:03:53.955
They began a bitter fight to shape Britain's exit from the EU according to their own agenda.

00:03:55.295 --> 00:03:56.896
They needed working class votes.

00:03:57.217 --> 00:04:01.060
They needed people who were angry at the system to win a referendum.

00:04:01.339 --> 00:04:03.801
But to construct a vision of post-Brexit

00:04:04.221 --> 00:04:06.242
Britain, they didn't need that anymore.

00:04:08.227 --> 00:04:09.730
Shortly after the referendum,

00:04:10.352 --> 00:04:11.353
optimism reigns.

00:04:13.056 --> 00:04:14.319
The new Prime Minister,

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:15.421
Theresa May,

00:04:15.802 --> 00:04:20.031
is confident that her government can deliver on the will of the people.

00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:25.502
Brexit means Brexit and we're going to make a success of it.

00:04:27.784 --> 00:04:31.246
For a long time this slogan remains empty of meaning.

00:04:31.906 --> 00:04:35.569
But soon it's time to give Brexit a more concrete definition.

00:04:36.189 --> 00:04:39.451
That I think is an important step forward and I'm pleased we're able to do it.

00:04:39.651 --> 00:04:44.955
The Minister from May's Cabinet has agreed to reveal to us what happened behind closed doors.

00:04:45.277 --> 00:04:51.119
David Gauke was a member of the government that was tasked with developing a detailed plan for Brexit.

00:04:51.739 --> 00:04:53.562
I served as one of the

00:04:54.424 --> 00:05:02.977
20 or so cabinet ministers that was around the table when we were making decisions as to how we tried to deliver Brexit in the best way possible.

00:05:04.023 --> 00:05:06.664
There were two incompatible objectives.

00:05:06.884 --> 00:05:11.346
One was to try to maintain good access to European markets.

00:05:11.426 --> 00:05:12.527
And then on the other hand,

00:05:12.587 --> 00:05:15.728
you had this desire to take control of our laws,

00:05:15.888 --> 00:05:18.649
completely unimpeded by the European Union.

00:05:19.449 --> 00:05:20.850
In summer 2018,

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.971
May called her cabinet to her countryside residence,

00:05:24.091 --> 00:05:24.552
Chequers,

00:05:24.912 --> 00:05:26.212
for decisive talks.

00:05:27.173 --> 00:05:31.935
It will be the site of the first showdown of a brutal inner party battle.

00:05:34.491 --> 00:05:36.432
I remember a sort of swelteringly hot day.

00:05:36.973 --> 00:05:39.214
We all arrived there in the morning.

00:05:39.795 --> 00:05:42.156
For May's then Foreign Minister Boris Johnson,

00:05:42.496 --> 00:05:46.579
it will be the last Cabinet meeting before he turns against his party leader.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.202
This was clearly going to be the big meeting.

00:05:50.562 --> 00:05:52.583
There was a lot of media build-up,

00:05:52.623 --> 00:05:53.344
there was a lot of talk,

00:05:53.384 --> 00:05:53.684
you know,

00:05:54.004 --> 00:05:54.885
will there be rows,

00:05:54.945 --> 00:05:56.025
will there be splits?

00:05:57.186 --> 00:05:57.887
At the meeting,

00:05:57.947 --> 00:06:01.529
Theresa May presents her Cabinet with a compromise solution.

00:06:05.879 --> 00:06:09.842
The Czechs plan would put an end to unlimited immigration from the EU,

00:06:10.422 --> 00:06:13.684
delivering on the most popular promise of the Brexit campaign.

00:06:14.805 --> 00:06:18.548
But she also wants to retain a close trade relationship with Europe,

00:06:18.968 --> 00:06:20.729
which would mean Britain having to stay in the EU.

00:06:20.949 --> 00:06:22.531
to many EU rules,

00:06:22.851 --> 00:06:24.512
a so-called soft Brexit.

00:06:25.993 --> 00:06:26.114
Well,

00:06:26.154 --> 00:06:27.655
in detailed discussions today,

00:06:27.815 --> 00:06:32.339
the Cabinet has agreed our collective position on the future of our negotiations with the EU,

00:06:32.899 --> 00:06:34.981
and I look forward to it being received positively.

00:06:37.323 --> 00:06:40.225
The EU's Brexit coordinator is relieved.

00:06:41.406 --> 00:06:43.168
Two years after the referendum,

00:06:43.508 --> 00:06:46.571
there's finally a concrete proposal on the table.

00:06:47.107 --> 00:06:48.829
The main message was we,

00:06:49.149 --> 00:06:50.130
from the British side,

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:53.854
want a reasonable agreement,

00:06:53.934 --> 00:06:54.875
not only on trade,

00:06:54.895 --> 00:06:57.418
but also in other fields,

00:06:57.478 --> 00:06:58.619
cooperation in other fields.

00:06:58.819 --> 00:07:05.045
There was a very constructive atmosphere in the beginning of these negotiations and certainly under Theresa May.

00:07:08.259 --> 00:07:12.262
But May's plan triggers a huge rebellion within her own party.

00:07:13.163 --> 00:07:14.504
For Tory hardliners,

00:07:14.764 --> 00:07:19.968
having to stick to EU regulations would be a betrayal of the Brexit referendum.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:22.930
In short,

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.312
we are going to be rules takers.

00:07:25.352 --> 00:07:27.754
We are going to be a de facto colony.

00:07:30.572 --> 00:07:35.000
A smooth and orderly exit is what business wants and I'm sure what citizens want up and down this country.

00:07:36.623 --> 00:07:40.109
The hardliners accuse May of surrendering to the EU.

00:07:41.231 --> 00:07:43.232
The House of Commons has never,

00:07:43.652 --> 00:07:45.232
ever surrendered to anybody,

00:07:45.492 --> 00:07:46.692
and it won't start now.

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:48.333
Within the Conservative Party,

00:07:48.853 --> 00:07:51.754
you already had this group called the European Research Group,

00:07:51.854 --> 00:07:52.514
the ERG,

00:07:53.134 --> 00:07:57.915
that was made up of those who were staunch Brexiteers.

00:07:58.696 --> 00:07:59.016
They were,

00:07:59.017 --> 00:07:59.976
in many respects,

00:08:00.056 --> 00:08:01.897
a party within a party.

00:08:02.417 --> 00:08:05.498
They were in no mood to compromise,

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:07.098
as far as they were concerned.

00:08:07.218 --> 00:08:10.099
Leaving the European Union meant we set our own laws,

00:08:10.179 --> 00:08:11.019
without exception.

00:08:11.319 --> 00:08:17.244
And so as things progressed in terms of the negotiations with the European Union,

00:08:17.724 --> 00:08:18.585
they just said,

00:08:18.645 --> 00:08:19.706
we don't support this deal.

00:08:20.246 --> 00:08:23.449
The leader of the ERG is Steve Baker,

00:08:24.129 --> 00:08:27.772
a radical neoliberal with close ties to the arms industry,

00:08:28.213 --> 00:08:29.094
who has supported,

00:08:29.294 --> 00:08:30.234
amongst other things,

00:08:30.575 --> 00:08:33.697
the deregulation of carcinogenic asbestos.

00:08:34.078 --> 00:08:40.903
A Brexit which requires a high degree of permanent alignment to the European Union will not go through this House of Commons.

00:08:41.663 --> 00:08:42.364
It will fail.

00:08:43.865 --> 00:08:47.086
Another key ERG figure is Jacob Rees-Mogg,

00:08:47.567 --> 00:08:50.308
a wealthy aristocrat and investment banker.

00:08:55.211 --> 00:08:59.313
But why are these men pushing for a hard Brexit at any cost?

00:09:00.313 --> 00:09:06.357
The economist Frances Coppola has investigated why this issue was so important to the rebels.

00:09:06.957 --> 00:09:10.299
She has looked into the backgrounds and connections behind this group.

00:09:11.419 --> 00:09:11.539
Well,

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:12.740
the European Research Group,

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:13.681
as it was known,

00:09:13.741 --> 00:09:16.442
although it wasn't very European and it didn't do any research,

00:09:16.883 --> 00:09:21.866
was essentially a group of very pro-Brexit and very right-wing

00:09:22.326 --> 00:09:26.189
Tories who are ideologically wedded to the idea of Britain as this...

00:09:26.329 --> 00:09:27.490
sort of free market,

00:09:27.851 --> 00:09:29.453
what they call buccaneering Britain,

00:09:29.513 --> 00:09:36.742
that would go around the world striking trade deals with everybody and being this kind of Singapore-Thames place that would be great for businesses.

00:09:37.142 --> 00:09:38.784
That term Singapore on Thames,

00:09:38.804 --> 00:09:39.425
what does that mean?

00:09:39.465 --> 00:09:40.987
I think Singapore...

00:09:41.287 --> 00:09:42.147
Singapore and Thames,

00:09:42.848 --> 00:09:43.128
yeah,

00:09:43.608 --> 00:09:44.728
it's this idea,

00:09:44.808 --> 00:09:45.289
I think,

00:09:45.869 --> 00:09:47.570
that we can be this,

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:47.910
like,

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:48.370
kind of

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:50.351
EU's offshore tax haven,

00:09:51.211 --> 00:10:00.435
this island off the coast of the EU that could kind of undercut them in terms of tax and regulation and be kind of,

00:10:00.715 --> 00:10:01.195
in a way,

00:10:01.775 --> 00:10:06.477
rather like the way in which Singapore and Hong Kong work in the Far East,

00:10:06.657 --> 00:10:10.639
this sort of centre for deregulated finance in particular.

00:10:16.788 --> 00:10:22.892
This radicalization of a number of Brexiteers was completely against the position they defended during the referendum.

00:10:22.912 --> 00:10:24.112
Because during the referendum it was,

00:10:24.212 --> 00:10:24.332
no,

00:10:25.233 --> 00:10:27.174
don't be afraid of a Brexit.

00:10:27.434 --> 00:10:29.255
It will never be a hard Brexit.

00:10:29.455 --> 00:10:31.737
So the idea of a hard Brexit came afterwards.

00:10:31.897 --> 00:10:38.781
And then there was the radical wing of the Tory party became always more radical.

00:10:39.661 --> 00:10:43.343
The best-known face of the radicals is Boris Johnson.

00:10:43.923 --> 00:10:45.764
At the Conservative Party conference,

00:10:45.964 --> 00:10:50.207
he makes no secret of his intention to try and take May's job.

00:10:50.707 --> 00:10:51.928
What will we hear from you today?

00:10:52.848 --> 00:10:53.869
All too soon,

00:10:53.929 --> 00:10:54.369
you'll hear.

00:10:55.309 --> 00:10:57.150
This is the moment to chuck checkers.

00:10:58.131 --> 00:11:00.012
But apart from his personal ambitions,

00:11:00.292 --> 00:11:03.274
whose interests is he pursuing with his Brexit agenda?

00:11:07.256 --> 00:11:09.517
We've never seen anybody who's raised as much.

00:11:09.517 --> 00:11:11.218
much money in British politics Boris Johnson.

00:11:11.479 --> 00:11:22.948
He's had record donations even just as a backbench MP so it wasn't a surprise to see Boris Johnson talking about deregulation to find Boris Johnson taking up the kind of positions that all these people wanted to see.

00:11:23.268 --> 00:11:31.154
It makes me utterly furious that a UK government could even contemplate such an arrangement.

00:11:32.475 --> 00:11:39.221
Research reveals that Johnson receives large donations from businessmen who want a relaxation of laws after.

00:11:39.521 --> 00:11:39.962
Brexit.

00:11:40.422 --> 00:11:45.766
One of his most important donors is the British construction equipment manufacturer JCB.

00:11:46.407 --> 00:11:52.992
This great company would have to accept whatever whimsical specifications Brussels comes up with.

00:11:53.312 --> 00:11:55.074
Does that seem like a good deal to you?

00:11:56.935 --> 00:11:57.516
I don't think so.

00:11:59.509 --> 00:12:00.450
JCB's owner,

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:01.270
Lord Bamford,

00:12:01.591 --> 00:12:04.873
is a billionaire and a member of the British House of Lords.

00:12:05.393 --> 00:12:05.894
My Lords,

00:12:06.054 --> 00:12:14.160
I'm a businessman and British businesses are certainly up to the task of making Britain a global leader in the free trade.

00:12:16.882 --> 00:12:19.884
He hopes for a better business environment after Brexit.

00:12:20.504 --> 00:12:21.125
Meanwhile,

00:12:21.185 --> 00:12:25.888
his shares in JCB sit in a tax haven in the Caribbean.

00:12:27.465 --> 00:12:28.045
When asked,

00:12:28.325 --> 00:12:34.767
JCB replies that the company and Lord Bamford have never made a secret of their political donations.

00:12:36.608 --> 00:12:44.311
How does a referendum won on popular promises turn into a campaign advocating a radical economic agenda?

00:12:45.551 --> 00:12:50.853
Guardian political journalist Sonia Soda has followed the rise of the hard Brexit faction.

00:12:53.165 --> 00:12:56.627
There's a very clear ideological driving force here,

00:12:57.128 --> 00:13:00.090
and that is that if you look historically in the UK,

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:05.693
the EU has been a force for sort of driving up welfare standards and workplace standards in the UK.

00:13:06.314 --> 00:13:06.434
Now,

00:13:06.474 --> 00:13:07.234
if you're on the right,

00:13:07.355 --> 00:13:07.915
you look at that.

00:13:07.935 --> 00:13:27.162
that and you just think god this is dreadful like we don't want workers to have these rights we don't want these standards to make food safe um we're interested in profit the political leaders of brexit are not people of the people men and women of the people they've been to these incredibly elite universities and

00:13:27.402 --> 00:13:36.345
i don't think that they are arguing for brexit out of a concern for um a lot of the people who voted for brexit let's say Thank you.

00:13:36.485 --> 00:13:41.769
I think the concept of Brexit that was about let's get back control of our borders,

00:13:41.829 --> 00:13:43.470
take back control of our fishing,

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:45.251
take back control of our manufacturing,

00:13:45.691 --> 00:13:47.432
be our state,

00:13:47.452 --> 00:13:48.473
be our people,

00:13:48.873 --> 00:13:50.755
which was kind of the original concept of Brexit in many,

00:13:50.795 --> 00:13:51.395
many people's minds.

00:13:51.415 --> 00:13:52.175
people's minds,

00:13:52.655 --> 00:13:54.416
kind of got hijacked by people who said,

00:13:54.436 --> 00:13:54.576
yeah,

00:13:54.577 --> 00:13:57.317
but actually the route to prosperity is to remove all regulation,

00:13:57.857 --> 00:13:58.737
cut taxes,

00:13:59.118 --> 00:14:01.378
and let's get out there and trade with everybody,

00:14:01.418 --> 00:14:02.859
and the EU is stopping us doing that.

00:14:03.279 --> 00:14:04.599
And it kind of got hijacked.

00:14:10.301 --> 00:14:10.842
Order

00:14:12.242 --> 00:14:12.542
Order

00:14:13.743 --> 00:14:17.284
When Theresa May puts her Brexit plan to a vote in Parliament,

00:14:17.684 --> 00:14:19.124
it's firmly rejected.

00:14:19.384 --> 00:14:20.545
The ayes to the right.

00:14:20.969 --> 00:14:21.910
202,

00:14:23.331 --> 00:14:24.551
the noes to the left

00:14:24.992 --> 00:14:26.493
432.

00:14:27.974 --> 00:14:29.235
So the noes have it,

00:14:29.535 --> 00:14:30.676
the noes have it.

00:14:31.216 --> 00:14:35.839
But what's driving so many Tory MPs to an unprecedented rebellion against the

00:14:35.839 --> 00:14:36.640
their party leader.

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:37.901
Conservative MPs,

00:14:37.982 --> 00:14:39.523
particularly pro-EU

00:14:40.204 --> 00:14:41.005
Conservative MPs,

00:14:41.065 --> 00:14:43.728
started reporting that their constituents were getting in touch with them,

00:14:44.149 --> 00:14:45.771
saying we want a hard Brexit.

00:14:46.111 --> 00:14:46.972
And in the British system,

00:14:47.113 --> 00:14:50.777
MPs are very reactive to their constituents because that's how you get into office.

00:14:51.097 --> 00:14:53.838
You need the people in your local constituency to vote for you.

00:14:54.158 --> 00:14:55.919
So if they're sending you messages,

00:14:56.199 --> 00:14:58.680
that's going to make a lot of swivering MPs think.

00:14:58.980 --> 00:14:59.520
But actually,

00:14:59.841 --> 00:15:07.324
what looked and was supposed to be a grassroots anger at the failure to leave the European Union was all really astroturf.

00:15:07.344 --> 00:15:08.724
It was fake grassroots.

00:15:08.725 --> 00:15:09.384
Lots and lots of adverse.

00:15:11.005 --> 00:15:13.046
Adverts started appearing on Facebook,

00:15:13.406 --> 00:15:19.109
targeted particularly at constituents of pro-EU Conservative MPs to send them messages to say,

00:15:19.169 --> 00:15:19.329
like,

00:15:19.509 --> 00:15:20.710
support the hard Brexit.

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:22.071
Flooding Facebook and then,

00:15:22.471 --> 00:15:22.951
in turn,

00:15:23.251 --> 00:15:24.152
flooding MPs'

00:15:24.272 --> 00:15:25.152
inboxes with this.

00:15:25.372 --> 00:15:28.474
These were adverts that were paid for by rich,

00:15:28.574 --> 00:15:29.455
rich donors,

00:15:29.955 --> 00:15:33.417
specifically to try and push the hard Brexit agenda.

00:15:38.005 --> 00:15:39.246
You influence debate,

00:15:39.666 --> 00:15:43.189
you put out adverts that will shift parliamentarians'

00:15:43.209 --> 00:15:43.610
views,

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:44.851
try and put pressure on them,

00:15:45.211 --> 00:15:47.893
and you do it in ways that it's anonymously funded.

00:15:47.894 --> 00:15:50.155
So the only thing that you're seeing is these messages.

00:15:53.298 --> 00:16:00.744
Investigations reveal that a million pounds worth of these adverts are managed by a company called CTF Partners.

00:16:01.725 --> 00:16:08.671
CTF Partners is a London-based lobby firm that worked on Boris Johnson's earlier campaigns for Mayor of London.

00:16:09.672 --> 00:16:16.077
The company also runs Facebook campaigns for the Saudi government and for fossil fuel companies.

00:16:17.898 --> 00:16:20.340
What is their role in the Brexit campaign?

00:16:21.641 --> 00:16:25.544
CTF Partners did not respond to a request for comment.

00:16:27.689 --> 00:16:31.712
But the battle for hard Brexit rages not just inside Parliament.

00:16:32.173 --> 00:16:33.294
On the streets of Britain,

00:16:33.314 --> 00:16:33.794
it seems,

00:16:34.094 --> 00:16:37.737
ordinary people are now rising up against May's compromise deal.

00:16:37.857 --> 00:16:40.620
Theresa May and our Parliament are traitors.

00:16:43.862 --> 00:16:47.125
At leave means leave marches and rallies across the country,

00:16:47.485 --> 00:16:51.468
May's deal is condemned as a betrayal of the true will of the people.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:56.212
But was this really an uprising of the people?

00:16:57.721 --> 00:17:00.584
Leave Means Leave is not a grassroots movement,

00:17:00.944 --> 00:17:02.746
but in reality a lobby firm,

00:17:03.127 --> 00:17:06.210
with huge budgets for flashy PR and loud rallies.

00:17:06.851 --> 00:17:09.433
The company is based at a building in Westminster,

00:17:09.934 --> 00:17:12.696
the former headquarters of the Vote Leave campaign,

00:17:13.117 --> 00:17:14.799
registered under the name

00:17:15.219 --> 00:17:16.240
Richard Tice.

00:17:19.721 --> 00:17:20.101
Tice,

00:17:20.462 --> 00:17:22.043
a millionaire property investor,

00:17:22.423 --> 00:17:26.265
was in the background of the Brexit campaign in 2016.

00:17:27.006 --> 00:17:27.746
And just look at it,

00:17:27.967 --> 00:17:29.147
even the weather's improved.

00:17:30.488 --> 00:17:34.491
He is one of the rich businessmen who have huge ambitions for Britain's

00:17:34.751 --> 00:17:35.311
EU exit.

00:17:35.531 --> 00:17:37.992
That's why he's battling for a hard Brexit.

00:17:38.292 --> 00:17:39.892
Are we all enjoying the sunshine,

00:17:39.893 --> 00:17:40.852
ladies and gentlemen?

00:17:42.093 --> 00:17:45.133
We know the sunshine's on the righteous.

00:17:45.593 --> 00:17:47.654
Richard Tice was one of those people that,

00:17:47.734 --> 00:17:49.114
during the Brexit referendum,

00:17:49.154 --> 00:17:52.335
was marginal in some respects.

00:17:52.795 --> 00:17:54.155
But after the referendum,

00:17:54.195 --> 00:17:54.415
actually,

00:17:54.475 --> 00:17:56.776
Tice became more and more a kind of central figure.

00:17:57.016 --> 00:17:58.096
And he set up this

00:17:58.756 --> 00:18:00.656
Leave Means Leave movement.

00:18:00.817 --> 00:18:02.137
They knew how to play the media.

00:18:02.577 --> 00:18:02.777
You know,

00:18:02.778 --> 00:18:03.357
a lot of you

00:18:03.417 --> 00:18:04.578
online campaigning,

00:18:04.938 --> 00:18:06.299
very hardline messaging,

00:18:06.599 --> 00:18:09.481
and kind of pushing this message you can't trust the politicians,

00:18:09.501 --> 00:18:12.362
you cannot trust the Conservatives to deliver Brexit for you.

00:18:12.662 --> 00:18:13.843
It was very populist,

00:18:13.863 --> 00:18:23.348
there was this idea that there was this terrible elite running Britain who were trying to thwart Brexit and that the masses should be very,

00:18:23.429 --> 00:18:24.729
very angry about it.

00:18:27.491 --> 00:18:28.171
Surprisingly,

00:18:28.331 --> 00:18:31.033
Richard Tice agreed immediately to meet us.

00:18:31.333 --> 00:18:33.335
in the office at his investment fund.

00:18:35.777 --> 00:18:38.099
We want to know what's driving him.

00:18:41.082 --> 00:18:44.565
When eventually there was what was called the Chequers Summit,

00:18:45.366 --> 00:18:48.409
we then realised that we were being betrayed.

00:18:48.669 --> 00:18:53.934
We were still going to be subservient slaves to the bureaucrats in Brussels.

00:18:54.494 --> 00:18:55.695
That's how revolutions begin.

00:18:56.116 --> 00:18:57.377
What's your vision for Brexit,

00:18:57.417 --> 00:18:57.537
then?

00:18:58.340 --> 00:18:59.803
So having a low tax,

00:19:00.263 --> 00:19:01.105
low regulation,

00:19:01.646 --> 00:19:02.648
high growth economy,

00:19:02.948 --> 00:19:04.050
just in my industry alone,

00:19:04.051 --> 00:19:04.270
I mean,

00:19:04.872 --> 00:19:06.454
which is real estate and construction,

00:19:06.595 --> 00:19:09.279
we've got all sorts of very time consuming,

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:11.704
very expensive EU rules on procurement.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:12.220
You know,

00:19:12.221 --> 00:19:15.723
this is the sort of stuff that I want to change in the way this country is run and governed.

00:19:16.364 --> 00:19:21.408
17.4 million We want the vote

00:19:22.088 --> 00:19:26.052
Traders to the country Traders to the country

00:19:27.653 --> 00:19:34.839
Richard Tice brings his old friend Nigel Farage out of retirement to become the face of leave means leave.

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:40.344
The former bond trader is able to turn the boring details of the Brexit process

00:19:40.704 --> 00:19:43.706
into populist slogans of national pride.

00:19:44.746 --> 00:19:48.669
This is about who we are as a nation.

00:19:49.029 --> 00:19:55.813
It's about our children and grandchildren growing up being proud to be

00:19:56.273 --> 00:19:59.295
British. We will get our independence back.

00:19:59.355 --> 00:20:03.357
We will get our pride and self-respect back.

00:20:03.457 --> 00:20:05.858
We are going to win

00:20:13.143 --> 00:20:14.764
It was a sort of political rock concert.

00:20:14.944 --> 00:20:15.624
We had colour,

00:20:15.644 --> 00:20:16.925
we had big backdrops,

00:20:17.106 --> 00:20:18.046
we had balloons,

00:20:18.066 --> 00:20:18.827
we had lights.

00:20:19.007 --> 00:20:19.407
You know,

00:20:19.408 --> 00:20:20.208
it was great.

00:20:20.528 --> 00:20:22.890
And people could see that something was happening,

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:26.372
but the establishment elite political class in Westminster,

00:20:26.773 --> 00:20:31.156
they didn't really wake up and smell the coffee and realise just quite what we were mobilising.

00:20:32.968 --> 00:20:35.390
The public were becoming increasingly polarised.

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:43.335
There was a growing sense by those who were most enthusiastic about leaving the European Union that this was all a plot,

00:20:43.395 --> 00:20:43.955
if you like,

00:20:44.035 --> 00:20:45.496
that they had been betrayed,

00:20:46.137 --> 00:20:47.798
that that metropolitan liberal...

00:20:47.918 --> 00:20:49.779
elite were ignoring them,

00:20:49.839 --> 00:20:51.420
that they weren't getting their way.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:54.181
And it was a very acrimonious time,

00:20:54.722 --> 00:20:55.682
not just in politics,

00:20:55.683 --> 00:20:57.723
but I think in the country as a whole.

00:20:58.123 --> 00:20:59.544
It was pretty poisonous.

00:21:00.144 --> 00:21:09.649
I've been in Brussels watching a British Prime Minister reducing our nation to a state of humiliation.

00:21:10.970 --> 00:21:14.192
If they think they can walk all over us...

00:21:15.172 --> 00:21:17.634
We're gonna march back and tell them no you can't,

00:21:18.034 --> 00:21:19.075
we will not give in.

00:21:19.455 --> 00:21:21.396
When people voted in 2016 though,

00:21:21.776 --> 00:21:24.198
it was mostly about immigration and the NHS.

00:21:24.358 --> 00:21:25.779
It wasn't about deregulation.

00:21:26.059 --> 00:21:26.179
No,

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:27.841
I don't accept that at all.

00:21:27.842 --> 00:21:28.501
Let's be very clear,

00:21:28.521 --> 00:21:29.522
what was on the ballot paper?

00:21:30.883 --> 00:21:31.083
And,

00:21:31.263 --> 00:21:31.484
you know,

00:21:31.524 --> 00:21:34.046
many of us who were promoting leave,

00:21:34.086 --> 00:21:35.127
particularly the business community,

00:21:35.568 --> 00:21:38.190
we knew the opportunities that were presented.

00:21:38.371 --> 00:21:38.891
Of course,

00:21:39.352 --> 00:21:40.893
different people who voted to leave,

00:21:41.254 --> 00:21:41.474
you know,

00:21:41.674 --> 00:21:43.516
voted for different reasons,

00:21:43.616 --> 00:21:47.140
but the key theme of it was taking back control.

00:21:48.221 --> 00:21:50.023
Do you think you'll get richer because of Brexit?

00:21:52.529 --> 00:21:53.469
Brexit's not about me.

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:55.871
I haven't thought of it in those terms.

00:21:56.431 --> 00:21:56.711
But

00:21:57.772 --> 00:22:00.034
I do believe Brexit does present opportunities.

00:22:00.194 --> 00:22:02.795
And if those opportunities lead to higher growth,

00:22:02.835 --> 00:22:03.476
then everybody,

00:22:03.816 --> 00:22:04.716
literally everybody,

00:22:05.337 --> 00:22:07.398
will be better off because of Brexit.

00:22:07.798 --> 00:22:09.179
And that includes all of us.

00:22:11.132 --> 00:22:14.334
We know that people don't want more deregulation.

00:22:14.434 --> 00:22:16.516
We've seen it time and again from surveys.

00:22:16.636 --> 00:22:18.577
They don't want less social spending.

00:22:18.617 --> 00:22:19.638
They want the opposite.

00:22:19.838 --> 00:22:21.059
They want higher taxes on the wealthy.

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:23.020
A lot of people in general want higher taxes.

00:22:23.320 --> 00:22:24.901
They want to see more regulation.

00:22:24.921 --> 00:22:25.962
They want a social net.

00:22:26.663 --> 00:22:36.878
But the way in which the Brexit referendum's aftermath has played out bears so little kind of resemblance to the popular anger that pushed it in the first place.

00:22:39.462 --> 00:22:40.203
Order

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:42.580
Under rising pressure,

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:45.381
May brings her deal before Parliament for a second time.

00:22:45.382 --> 00:22:46.562
The no's to the left,

00:22:46.742 --> 00:22:48.582
391.

00:22:48.943 --> 00:22:49.983
And fails again.

00:22:50.723 --> 00:22:51.824
So the no's have it,

00:22:52.084 --> 00:22:53.084
the no's have it.

00:22:53.224 --> 00:22:55.465
There wasn't a majority for a hard Brexit,

00:22:56.105 --> 00:22:58.566
and there wasn't a majority for a soft Brexit.

00:22:58.766 --> 00:23:01.287
There was a blocking majority against every option,

00:23:01.967 --> 00:23:03.528
and we were essentially stuck.

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:09.807
The hard Brexit movement argued that this didn't need to be so complicated.

00:23:10.609 --> 00:23:16.878
Britain should simply leave the EU and stand proudly alone as a sovereign independent country,

00:23:17.699 --> 00:23:19.622
with no negative consequences.

00:23:21.512 --> 00:23:26.355
The promise was Britain should be a sovereign country like it was in the days of empire.

00:23:26.455 --> 00:23:28.076
This idea of take back control.

00:23:28.577 --> 00:23:33.480
Now the whole issue with that is that that notion of national sovereignty doesn't really exist anymore in a

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:34.601
21st century world.

00:23:35.001 --> 00:23:36.382
So in a world that's more global.

00:23:37.103 --> 00:23:37.783
where there is far,

00:23:37.843 --> 00:23:38.924
far more trade,

00:23:39.344 --> 00:23:39.585
you know,

00:23:39.885 --> 00:23:41.606
the world is becoming more interconnected.

00:23:41.886 --> 00:23:43.808
But that has never been acknowledged.

00:23:43.848 --> 00:23:50.974
The problem with being integrated into something like the EU for over 40 years is that all your business processes,

00:23:50.994 --> 00:23:52.235
your supply chains,

00:23:52.595 --> 00:23:53.676
the way you do things,

00:23:53.816 --> 00:23:58.139
all sorts of things are deeply integrated with that.

00:23:58.239 --> 00:24:03.043
So unpicking that is a bit like kind of trying to unscramble an omelette.

00:24:04.524 --> 00:24:07.386
But soon the complicated reality becomes clear.

00:24:08.127 --> 00:24:17.653
Guy Verhofstadt warns that there is an inevitable trade-off between Britain's freedom to change its own rules and continued access to EU trade.

00:24:18.133 --> 00:24:19.314
If you want a trade deal with the...

00:24:19.314 --> 00:24:20.135
the European Union,

00:24:20.756 --> 00:24:21.997
like in every trade deal,

00:24:22.498 --> 00:24:22.938
we will,

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:24.821
as Europeans,

00:24:25.762 --> 00:24:26.803
defend our interests.

00:24:27.063 --> 00:24:27.203
Look,

00:24:27.744 --> 00:24:29.666
we agree to have no quotas,

00:24:30.127 --> 00:24:32.189
no tariffs when we are talking about trade,

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:33.551
but you have to accept...

00:24:34.272 --> 00:24:35.393
a level playing field.

00:24:35.613 --> 00:24:40.557
It's a word that we use every day in these negotiations.

00:24:41.017 --> 00:24:45.721
But a level playing field means that there is a fair competition between us and them,

00:24:46.041 --> 00:24:46.682
state aid,

00:24:46.802 --> 00:24:47.543
climate change,

00:24:47.663 --> 00:24:48.984
social rules and so on.

00:24:49.564 --> 00:24:50.405
The British industry,

00:24:50.885 --> 00:24:52.467
like also the European industry,

00:24:52.847 --> 00:24:57.210
has an interest in having a good trade relationship.

00:24:57.651 --> 00:25:04.096
Most experts predict huge damage for the British economy if Britain loses direct access to EU money.

00:25:04.096 --> 00:25:04.416
markets.

00:25:05.037 --> 00:25:10.584
But the supporters of hard Brexit find one man who is prepared to say the opposite.

00:25:11.345 --> 00:25:12.146
In our research,

00:25:12.167 --> 00:25:16.072
we stumble over a little-known trade expert called Shankar Singham.

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:18.171
If you look at this problem for long enough,

00:25:18.172 --> 00:25:22.494
I think all the technical experts ultimately come to the same conclusion.

00:25:22.914 --> 00:25:23.154
You know,

00:25:23.234 --> 00:25:23.895
Shankar said,

00:25:24.115 --> 00:25:24.855
I've got a plan.

00:25:25.116 --> 00:25:31.660
And that was really important because you had economists and trade people lining up around the street after Britain left the European Union.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:31.880
Union,

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:32.922
tell them it was a bad idea.

00:25:33.402 --> 00:25:35.384
Here comes this guy who'd been to Oxford,

00:25:35.425 --> 00:25:36.225
who'd worked in America.

00:25:36.366 --> 00:25:37.467
He's telling you it's a good idea.

00:25:37.887 --> 00:25:41.552
And he became the kind of the trade guru for the Brexiteers.

00:25:41.812 --> 00:25:42.032
Well,

00:25:42.053 --> 00:25:45.417
it's been interesting watching the rise and rise of Shankar Singham because,

00:25:45.577 --> 00:25:46.157
you know,

00:25:46.218 --> 00:25:46.618
he's actually...

00:25:46.778 --> 00:25:48.380
not that knowledgeable on trade.

00:25:48.381 --> 00:25:51.022
There are people out there who know much more about trade than he does,

00:25:51.342 --> 00:25:53.024
but they weren't elevated in the same way.

00:25:53.324 --> 00:25:59.310
And I can only conclude that it's because the guys who really knew about trade weren't saying the right things.

00:25:59.470 --> 00:25:59.850
They were saying,

00:25:59.851 --> 00:25:59.991
well,

00:26:00.011 --> 00:26:00.311
actually,

00:26:00.551 --> 00:26:02.353
there aren't any simple solutions.

00:26:02.813 --> 00:26:04.815
I think what Schenker was doing was saying,

00:26:05.396 --> 00:26:05.516
well,

00:26:05.517 --> 00:26:06.176
if we just do this,

00:26:06.177 --> 00:26:07.458
we can have a simple solution.

00:26:08.459 --> 00:26:11.962
Schenker-Singham soon becomes known as the Brexiteer's brain.

00:26:13.102 --> 00:26:13.763
He writes a

00:26:14.864 --> 00:26:19.128
150-page report in which he claims that the repeal of EU workplace,

00:26:19.248 --> 00:26:25.754
environmental and agricultural regulations will prove a major boost to the British economy.

00:26:27.075 --> 00:26:30.558
The ultra-conservative members of the European Research Group,

00:26:30.939 --> 00:26:32.440
including Jacob Rees-Mogg,

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:38.406
celebrate Singham's so-called Plan A-plus as an alternative to May's Chequers deal.

00:26:39.574 --> 00:26:43.775
This is about how you have a fantastic Brexit.

00:26:43.776 --> 00:26:46.296
It's a really exciting and good paper.

00:26:46.297 --> 00:26:49.317
But who is Singham and who is financing his work?

00:26:50.337 --> 00:26:55.538
Singham works for an organisation with the innocuous name the Institute of Economic Affairs.

00:26:56.499 --> 00:26:59.780
The IEA is a neoliberal think tank which supports,

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:00.900
amongst other ideas,

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:07.922
the privatisation of the NHS as well as defending the deregulation of the tobacco industry and tax havens.

00:27:08.474 --> 00:27:11.296
The think tank also supports a hard Brexit.

00:27:11.896 --> 00:27:16.318
The think tank world was really influential in making the idea of a hard Brexit,

00:27:16.399 --> 00:27:19.360
making that seem like something that was really achievable and a good thing.

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:22.622
Bringing what were really fringe issues in British politics,

00:27:22.662 --> 00:27:24.103
deregulation and things like that,

00:27:24.383 --> 00:27:25.804
and making them mainstream.

00:27:27.945 --> 00:27:32.067
We want to know whose interests are really being pushed by these think tanks.

00:27:33.348 --> 00:27:36.190
We find a former insider who's prepared to tell us.

00:27:40.714 --> 00:27:45.215
Shamir Sunny was employed at a think tank that works closely with the IEA.

00:27:46.316 --> 00:27:54.258
He was enraged by what he experienced firsthand and decided to speak out on how these free market think tanks really work.

00:27:55.278 --> 00:28:00.040
It's an enormous network of right-wing lobby groups that are lobby groups,

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:00.940
not think tanks.

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:04.421
I know the last organization that I worked for,

00:28:04.741 --> 00:28:06.541
a donor would come in and be like,

00:28:06.601 --> 00:28:06.862
hey,

00:28:06.902 --> 00:28:07.462
I really want to,

00:28:07.822 --> 00:28:08.102
you know.

00:28:08.558 --> 00:28:11.580
I need help with whatever my salmon farms or whatever.

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:16.383
And that lobby group will create a report around that sort of incentive from the donor.

00:28:16.503 --> 00:28:27.531
And then those reports are then taken by these think tanks to politicians and presented as legitimate policy suggestions,

00:28:28.632 --> 00:28:28.932
going,

00:28:29.112 --> 00:28:29.993
this is good for you,

00:28:30.953 --> 00:28:32.134
people really want this,

00:28:32.794 --> 00:28:33.955
the public really desire this,

00:28:33.956 --> 00:28:35.116
and it's good for the country.

00:28:35.716 --> 00:28:37.798
When most of the time it's purely because they've...

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:40.410
This is the incentive of their donors.

00:28:40.511 --> 00:28:41.671
If you're a corporation,

00:28:41.672 --> 00:28:42.451
if you think about it,

00:28:42.911 --> 00:28:43.171
you know,

00:28:43.231 --> 00:28:43.711
what do you do?

00:28:43.831 --> 00:28:45.092
You've got £50,000.

00:28:45.132 --> 00:28:47.332
Do you give it to a lobbyist that's registered?

00:28:48.193 --> 00:28:48.973
Won't get you very far,

00:28:49.093 --> 00:28:49.953
might get you one meeting,

00:28:50.113 --> 00:28:50.913
it'll be registered,

00:28:51.093 --> 00:28:51.894
people will know about it.

00:28:52.114 --> 00:28:54.314
Or do you give it to a think tank to write a report?

00:28:54.614 --> 00:28:56.075
The report will get into the media,

00:28:56.215 --> 00:28:57.575
your issues will get all the way out there,

00:28:57.755 --> 00:28:59.436
and your name won't be anywhere near it.

00:29:00.716 --> 00:29:02.057
In summer 2018,

00:29:02.397 --> 00:29:07.018
a journalist from Greenpeace went undercover in the Institute of Economic Affairs.

00:29:07.362 --> 00:29:13.227
to find out how think tanks offer their corporate funders influence in the Brexit process.

00:29:14.668 --> 00:29:27.879
The journalist poses as an investor in US agriculture who is hoping for a relaxation of British food standards post-Brexit in order that his hormone-treated beef can be sold on the UK market.

00:29:31.702 --> 00:29:34.444
He is invited to a meeting by the head of the IEA,

00:29:34.984 --> 00:29:35.865
Mark Littlewood,

00:29:36.205 --> 00:29:37.006
to explore

00:29:37.282 --> 00:29:38.983
what collaboration might look like.

00:29:41.204 --> 00:29:41.664
Delighted.

00:29:42.004 --> 00:29:43.124
Love this meeting.

00:29:43.145 --> 00:29:43.285
Very,

00:29:43.286 --> 00:29:43.565
very good.

00:29:43.665 --> 00:29:43.965
Thanks.

00:29:44.325 --> 00:29:52.148
Littlewood offers the journalist the chance to fund a report that will help influence British agricultural policy post-Brexit.

00:29:53.349 --> 00:29:54.509
If we fully funded this,

00:29:57.150 --> 00:29:57.851
would you be able to...

00:29:57.931 --> 00:29:58.191
I mean,

00:30:00.692 --> 00:30:00.952
you know,

00:30:01.592 --> 00:30:05.314
would there be some ability to kind of make sure that they're covering the areas of...

00:30:05.810 --> 00:30:06.570
specific interest?

00:30:06.571 --> 00:30:07.171
Oh sure,

00:30:07.311 --> 00:30:07.811
absolutely.

00:30:08.972 --> 00:30:12.053
I don't mind our donors affecting us on salience,

00:30:12.293 --> 00:30:14.515
so when we won't go as far as it's been said,

00:30:14.615 --> 00:30:16.055
we need you to reach this conclusion.

00:30:16.215 --> 00:30:16.436
Sure,

00:30:16.516 --> 00:30:17.516
not necessarily the conclusion,

00:30:17.616 --> 00:30:18.857
but to make sure that our...

00:30:18.858 --> 00:30:20.017
That is substantial content,

00:30:20.198 --> 00:30:20.438
fine.

00:30:21.618 --> 00:30:23.459
And that it's prominent and...

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:27.301
That would not be a worry,

00:30:27.302 --> 00:30:27.401
yeah.

00:30:27.402 --> 00:30:27.942
We can do that.

00:30:29.370 --> 00:30:36.298
There is no way this report is going to say the most important thing we need to do is to keep American beef out of our market in order to pop up our beef butlers.

00:30:36.438 --> 00:30:37.780
It's exactly the opposite.

00:30:37.880 --> 00:30:38.080
Okay,

00:30:38.561 --> 00:30:38.941
fine.

00:30:40.060 --> 00:30:43.102
The Institute for Economic Affairs has always run on private money.

00:30:43.522 --> 00:30:45.103
They will not say where their money comes from,

00:30:45.143 --> 00:30:46.444
but we know it's private money.

00:30:47.065 --> 00:30:51.388
Occasionally we'll get a little bit of an insight into just where some of that money might come from.

00:30:51.468 --> 00:30:53.789
So in the past they've got money from BP,

00:30:54.049 --> 00:30:54.970
so from the oil industry.

00:30:55.430 --> 00:30:56.511
from the gambling industry,

00:30:56.871 --> 00:30:59.252
from people involved with the offshore financial industry.

00:30:59.272 --> 00:31:04.454
So really corporate interests who have very specific agendas.

00:31:04.674 --> 00:31:19.259
In Britain there's such a huge crisis of transparency because all of these organisations don't reveal their donors and the public are not aware of who is funding the policies that government officials are enacting or bringing forward.

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:22.480
If you want access to government,

00:31:23.521 --> 00:31:28.302
put some money into these right-wing think tanks and then watch the entire country burn.

00:31:29.442 --> 00:31:31.083
Confronted with these statements,

00:31:31.383 --> 00:31:32.623
the IEA tells us,

00:31:33.464 --> 00:31:36.364
we do not act in the interests of our donors,

00:31:36.764 --> 00:31:41.706
except to the extent that they are interested in free trade and free markets.

00:31:42.166 --> 00:31:47.467
The IEA makes independent editorial decisions and then seeks funding.

00:31:48.048 --> 00:31:49.708
It is certainly not controversial.

00:31:49.868 --> 00:31:53.892
that the IEA's principles are aligned with the interests of our donors.

00:31:54.592 --> 00:31:59.237
Boris Johnson has publicly praised IEA reports on several occasions.

00:31:59.837 --> 00:32:02.400
I've just seen this stuff that's been done for the IEA,

00:32:02.401 --> 00:32:04.341
I think that's been talked about this morning,

00:32:04.342 --> 00:32:05.502
and looking at it,

00:32:05.503 --> 00:32:07.284
I think it's a very good piece of work.

00:32:07.384 --> 00:32:09.446
It allows us properly to take...

00:32:09.526 --> 00:32:11.647
back control of our laws,

00:32:11.687 --> 00:32:13.088
which you can't do under Chequers.

00:32:13.148 --> 00:32:13.669
Under Chequers,

00:32:13.709 --> 00:32:16.330
you lose control of your laws.

00:32:16.711 --> 00:32:17.971
Your laws are made in Brussels,

00:32:18.372 --> 00:32:20.193
and they're imposed on the U.K.

00:32:20.333 --> 00:32:20.573
I mean,

00:32:21.093 --> 00:32:23.895
we've never had anything like that in a thousand years.

00:32:23.955 --> 00:32:25.816
So this is a very exciting way forward.

00:32:26.877 --> 00:32:31.400
But there is a very different major obstacle to a hard break with the EU,

00:32:32.341 --> 00:32:36.824
one that threatens to confront Britain with its recent bloody past.

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:44.181
Until 20 years ago,

00:32:44.621 --> 00:32:54.046
a brutal civil war raged in Northern Ireland between those who wanted to remain part of the United Kingdom and those who wanted to join the Republic of Ireland.

00:32:55.446 --> 00:32:58.208
On Good Friday in 1998,

00:32:58.848 --> 00:33:00.229
a peace agreement was signed.

00:33:00.989 --> 00:33:05.711
It promised open borders without controls between North and South.

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:11.083
That makes part of the peace in Northern Ireland,

00:33:11.084 --> 00:33:12.864
the fact that there is no longer a border.

00:33:13.745 --> 00:33:17.689
So for us it was very important that this Good Friday Agreement,

00:33:17.749 --> 00:33:21.352
that the peace in Northern Ireland is secured.

00:33:21.353 --> 00:33:24.674
And to safeguard also this economic space,

00:33:24.975 --> 00:33:27.677
because that's one economic space today,

00:33:28.298 --> 00:33:29.118
Ireland and

00:33:29.539 --> 00:33:30.600
Northern Ireland.

00:33:32.021 --> 00:33:35.244
But a hard Brexit would threaten the promise of no border.

00:33:36.176 --> 00:33:38.699
If the UK deviates from EU rules,

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:41.263
border controls on trade would be necessary.

00:33:43.126 --> 00:33:47.772
Hard Brexit advocates need a solution that will work even if there is a complete

00:33:47.952 --> 00:33:48.913
break with the EU.

00:33:49.893 --> 00:33:51.654
Another job for Shankar Singham,

00:33:52.054 --> 00:33:56.417
the man who seems to succeed in finding solutions to impossible problems.

00:33:57.357 --> 00:33:59.498
He is dispatched to Northern Ireland.

00:34:00.459 --> 00:34:00.919
His trip,

00:34:01.119 --> 00:34:01.739
we discover,

00:34:02.060 --> 00:34:04.081
was paid for by Prosperity UK,

00:34:05.101 --> 00:34:08.463
a private company run by a millionaire hedge fund boss.

00:34:09.303 --> 00:34:10.044
At the airport,

00:34:10.124 --> 00:34:11.845
Singham is met by Stephen Kelly,

00:34:12.405 --> 00:34:15.227
chairman of the Northern Ireland Confederation of industry.

00:34:16.147 --> 00:34:17.768
Kelly tells us how this means.

00:34:17.768 --> 00:34:18.889
meeting with Singham went.

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:20.090
First,

00:34:20.430 --> 00:34:23.493
he shows him the invisible border between Northern Ireland,

00:34:23.853 --> 00:34:24.534
part of the UK,

00:34:25.274 --> 00:34:26.956
and the Republic of Ireland.

00:34:27.676 --> 00:34:28.597
So the road we're on now,

00:34:28.837 --> 00:34:32.200
there's dozens of informal crossings through farmland,

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:33.421
through laneways.

00:34:33.841 --> 00:34:36.143
There's even a crossing within someone's house.

00:34:37.164 --> 00:34:37.525
Already,

00:34:38.145 --> 00:34:39.246
we've crossed the border once,

00:34:39.546 --> 00:34:41.428
and we've just crossed it again.

00:34:41.968 --> 00:34:43.209
So the house is on the right-hand side,

00:34:43.210 --> 00:34:43.850
we're in the Republic,

00:34:43.910 --> 00:34:46.232
but now this business on the right-hand side is in...

00:34:47.096 --> 00:34:47.656
Northern Ireland,

00:34:48.337 --> 00:34:49.017
here we go again.

00:34:49.337 --> 00:34:50.838
Just as the road changes colour here,

00:34:51.478 --> 00:34:52.738
that's the border one more time.

00:34:53.479 --> 00:34:56.020
And this business is now in Southern Ireland,

00:34:57.120 --> 00:34:58.901
but it has a gate out into

00:34:59.341 --> 00:34:59.921
Northern Ireland,

00:35:00.482 --> 00:35:03.023
and we're crossing the border once again,

00:35:03.363 --> 00:35:03.823
just now.

00:35:04.623 --> 00:35:08.745
Kelly shows his visitor from London this abandoned customs facility,

00:35:09.365 --> 00:35:13.367
the only remaining visible sign of the border that used to exist here.

00:35:14.408 --> 00:35:15.128
Singham's mission?

00:35:15.696 --> 00:35:19.419
Could goods be controlled without the need for physical checkpoints like this?

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:21.601
Their trip was largely about,

00:35:21.841 --> 00:35:23.603
is there technology that could be put in play?

00:35:24.984 --> 00:35:29.007
How do we continue to have that frictionless trade that we have at the moment?

00:35:29.287 --> 00:35:35.572
From blockchain to satellites and drones and stuff being embedded in the road,

00:35:35.733 --> 00:35:38.775
linked to barcodes and a whole raft of...

00:35:39.916 --> 00:35:44.279
Wild and wacky suggestions all ends up in one place which is nothing,

00:35:44.799 --> 00:35:45.360
nothing will work.

00:35:46.781 --> 00:35:53.345
So I brought them into this very spot and I set them the challenge that whatever work they were doing on whatever alternative arrangements,

00:35:54.046 --> 00:35:55.226
it needed to meet this test.

00:35:55.507 --> 00:36:08.355
It needed to meet the fact that that person in that house 100 yards away and that person in the house another 100 yards away need to have their lives transacted in exactly the same way after Brexit as they do before.

00:36:09.576 --> 00:36:11.457
I don't think Shanker or others really understood it.

00:36:20.042 --> 00:36:22.404
So the concern is if a border is reintroduced,

00:36:22.764 --> 00:36:27.307
that partition on this island of Ireland becomes physical and manifest once again.

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:34.131
This is a memorial to...

00:36:35.892 --> 00:36:40.136
13 people who were shot dead by the British Army in Derry on the

00:36:40.616 --> 00:36:45.721
31st of January 1972 in an event which has been known internationally as

00:36:46.121 --> 00:36:46.641
Bloody Sunday.

00:36:47.362 --> 00:36:49.684
But it's also the place where peace was born as well.

00:36:51.005 --> 00:36:59.933
This is the thing that's so frustrating to people here is that people far removed from here think that this is just about lines in the road,

00:37:01.534 --> 00:37:02.615
spreadsheets around.

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:07.342
Trading widgets and food.

00:37:08.263 --> 00:37:08.763
But it's not...

00:37:09.563 --> 00:37:11.625
This is our daily life.

00:37:12.825 --> 00:37:13.726
We want to live in peace.

00:37:14.666 --> 00:37:17.088
And because Brexit doesn't have the consent of people here,

00:37:17.628 --> 00:37:22.231
that really runs the risk of actually reigniting the troubles that we had back 25 years ago.

00:37:29.628 --> 00:37:32.270
Just one week after his visit to Northern Ireland,

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:35.552
Singham presents his privately funded report in London.

00:37:36.312 --> 00:37:42.276
This plan ignores the concerns of his Northern Ireland host and includes proposals for border controls.

00:37:43.337 --> 00:37:45.878
Singham did not respond to our request for comment.

00:37:46.359 --> 00:37:46.899
In the past,

00:37:46.939 --> 00:37:47.339
however,

00:37:47.719 --> 00:37:54.624
he has emphasised that he does not find it unusual for a think tank to become actively involved in politics.

00:37:55.820 --> 00:38:00.464
Singham's report has a huge influence on the stance of the conservative hardliners,

00:38:00.964 --> 00:38:03.186
especially the European Research Group.

00:38:03.987 --> 00:38:08.130
The ERG weren't really engaging with some of these practical problems.

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:11.253
They weren't recognising what some of the risks were.

00:38:11.493 --> 00:38:23.403
They were just dismissing what were real problems and failing to recognise that there would be consequences if we didn't properly address the issue of the Irish border.

00:38:24.876 --> 00:38:25.736
The Rebels win.

00:38:26.817 --> 00:38:31.038
Theresa May's deal is rejected for a third and final time.

00:38:32.058 --> 00:38:32.619
Order

00:38:34.079 --> 00:38:36.580
Order. The ayes to the right,

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:38.281
286.

00:38:39.361 --> 00:38:40.801
The noes to the left,

00:38:41.182 --> 00:38:42.862
344.

00:38:45.103 --> 00:38:46.263
So the noes have it,

00:38:46.483 --> 00:38:47.484
the noes have it.

00:38:48.672 --> 00:38:53.335
This result means that the UK's exit from the EU will be further delayed,

00:38:53.955 --> 00:38:59.438
creating the absurd situation that the UK will have to participate in the EU election,

00:38:59.798 --> 00:39:01.559
even though it wants to leave the EU.

00:39:02.380 --> 00:39:06.962
This gives the hardliners the perfect chance for a final attack on May's government.

00:39:08.103 --> 00:39:09.603
I think for a long time,

00:39:09.844 --> 00:39:11.605
in that strange interregnum,

00:39:11.606 --> 00:39:15.267
in that strange period where there was all these battles going on in the Commons,

00:39:15.687 --> 00:39:17.728
people were wondering what Nigel Farage would do next.

00:39:23.184 --> 00:39:26.387
And we knew that the mood of the country was rapidly coming our way,

00:39:26.667 --> 00:39:27.167
big time.

00:39:27.528 --> 00:39:30.270
So it's all about timing and politics is all about timing.

00:39:30.610 --> 00:39:31.391
And Nigel said,

00:39:31.911 --> 00:39:33.072
the time is nearly right.

00:39:34.013 --> 00:39:40.298
Richard Theiss and Nigel Farage found the Brexit party to participate in the EU election.

00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:47.124
The party styles itself as a radical alternative to the Conservatives.

00:39:47.904 --> 00:39:49.846
It has only one political goal,

00:39:50.386 --> 00:39:52.048
the hardest possible Brexit.

00:39:53.648 --> 00:39:56.450
But our research shows that the Brexit party,

00:39:56.751 --> 00:39:59.093
which claims to represent the people on the street,

00:39:59.473 --> 00:40:01.134
is anything but a people's party.

00:40:01.895 --> 00:40:02.736
The party is,

00:40:02.796 --> 00:40:03.316
in fact,

00:40:03.596 --> 00:40:05.338
the limited company run by

00:40:05.798 --> 00:40:06.959
Farage and Theis.

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:10.542
This is not the way political parties are normally run.

00:40:11.043 --> 00:40:11.383
Normally,

00:40:11.403 --> 00:40:13.505
if you become a member of a political party,

00:40:13.765 --> 00:40:14.406
it's one member,

00:40:14.486 --> 00:40:14.946
one vote,

00:40:14.947 --> 00:40:16.467
and you get to have a say in the party.

00:40:16.848 --> 00:40:17.749
With the Brexit party,

00:40:17.750 --> 00:40:19.750
and the language they use is really interesting too,

00:40:19.751 --> 00:40:20.671
they didn't talk about members,

00:40:20.672 --> 00:40:21.632
they talked about supporters.

00:40:22.088 --> 00:40:24.109
So what would happen was you'd go on to your PayPal,

00:40:24.409 --> 00:40:26.510
you'd give the Brexit Party as much money as you wanted,

00:40:26.511 --> 00:40:28.171
and you'd become a registered supporter.

00:40:28.611 --> 00:40:33.013
But that didn't give you any more say in the Brexit Party than I have or anybody else.

00:40:33.673 --> 00:40:36.214
And the digital democracy that they were promising,

00:40:36.215 --> 00:40:38.055
and this is the words that Farage was using,

00:40:38.255 --> 00:40:39.816
kind of a new form of democracy.

00:40:40.296 --> 00:40:42.477
Once you looked at it in any kind of depth,

00:40:42.478 --> 00:40:42.917
it was really,

00:40:42.977 --> 00:40:43.517
really hollow.

00:40:43.537 --> 00:40:44.537
So they had like an app,

00:40:44.637 --> 00:40:46.518
but the app didn't really engage people at all.

00:40:46.519 --> 00:40:47.998
I just kind of let them make comments.

00:40:48.198 --> 00:40:52.559
The Brexit Party isn't really a political party.

00:40:52.779 --> 00:40:53.540
It's a company,

00:40:53.940 --> 00:40:54.100
right?

00:40:54.160 --> 00:40:54.280
Yeah,

00:40:54.380 --> 00:40:54.500
we,

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:54.900
you know,

00:40:55.780 --> 00:40:57.121
things were moving so quickly.

00:40:57.581 --> 00:40:57.801
You know,

00:40:57.802 --> 00:40:59.121
we just didn't have time to set up,

00:40:59.981 --> 00:41:00.221
you know,

00:41:00.802 --> 00:41:02.922
a political party in the traditional sense of the word.

00:41:03.442 --> 00:41:03.562
So,

00:41:03.862 --> 00:41:04.103
you know,

00:41:04.143 --> 00:41:05.203
Nigel knew the right thing to do.

00:41:05.283 --> 00:41:06.063
Set it up as a company.

00:41:06.083 --> 00:41:06.603
There's no reason,

00:41:06.663 --> 00:41:06.883
you know,

00:41:06.884 --> 00:41:08.224
you're allowed to do that under the rules.

00:41:08.584 --> 00:41:09.344
That's what we did.

00:41:09.544 --> 00:41:10.564
So we both led from the front.

00:41:10.645 --> 00:41:10.905
Nigel,

00:41:10.906 --> 00:41:11.665
the campaigner,

00:41:12.165 --> 00:41:12.425
you know,

00:41:12.426 --> 00:41:13.366
the recognised voice.

00:41:13.426 --> 00:41:15.247
I wasn't really known then as an individual,

00:41:15.767 --> 00:41:16.427
as a person.

00:41:16.607 --> 00:41:16.847
You know,

00:41:16.848 --> 00:41:17.188
people say,

00:41:17.228 --> 00:41:17.588
Richard who?

00:41:18.808 --> 00:41:20.009
But I was the organiser,

00:41:20.369 --> 00:41:23.551
and what I've got is an ability to make rapid-fire decisions.

00:41:23.971 --> 00:41:24.211
And so,

00:41:24.251 --> 00:41:24.431
yeah,

00:41:25.532 --> 00:41:26.572
we just mobilised,

00:41:26.832 --> 00:41:29.493
and it was just constantly decision after decision,

00:41:29.553 --> 00:41:30.014
just like that.

00:41:30.334 --> 00:41:30.574
You know,

00:41:30.654 --> 00:41:33.415
because we were running basically a committee of two,

00:41:33.876 --> 00:41:34.576
it was very easy.

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:39.482
Although there are many small supporter contributions to the Brexit party,

00:41:40.022 --> 00:41:45.404
our research shows that they are dwarfed by inflows from wealthy donors.

00:41:46.224 --> 00:41:47.485
So people like Jeremy Hoskins,

00:41:47.505 --> 00:41:50.646
who's a hedge fund guy who used to fund the Leave campaign,

00:41:50.666 --> 00:41:51.267
the referendum,

00:41:51.268 --> 00:41:52.367
had funded the Conservative Party,

00:41:52.627 --> 00:41:55.168
he donated to the Brexit party quite a lot of money.

00:41:55.428 --> 00:41:56.769
A man called Christopher Harbourn,

00:41:56.829 --> 00:41:58.770
who'd previously given some money to Conservatives,

00:41:59.130 --> 00:42:02.771
he donated well over £10 million to the Brexit party.

00:42:02.851 --> 00:42:04.032
It's huge sums of money.

00:42:04.592 --> 00:42:09.276
for a political party.

00:42:09.337 --> 00:42:10.257
Thank you

00:42:12.679 --> 00:42:14.081
Thank you Thank you Thank you Oh,

00:42:14.082 --> 00:42:14.801
we got this sorted.

00:42:14.802 --> 00:42:16.103
With their radical message,

00:42:16.223 --> 00:42:21.487
Thais and Farage are robbing the conservatives of donors and voters.

00:42:22.308 --> 00:42:25.110
Nigel Farage is an outsider to the system.

00:42:25.291 --> 00:42:27.833
He sort of portrays himself as this,

00:42:27.893 --> 00:42:28.113
you know,

00:42:28.273 --> 00:42:30.195
figure who speaks truth to power,

00:42:30.255 --> 00:42:30.796
who keeps,

00:42:30.797 --> 00:42:31.036
you know...

00:42:31.356 --> 00:42:33.157
British prime ministers honest.

00:42:33.317 --> 00:42:38.258
And the role that he has always played is to put pressure on conservative leaders,

00:42:38.638 --> 00:42:43.059
to scare them that he's going to take votes away from them and that they need to give him what he wants.

00:42:43.579 --> 00:42:44.460
The Brexit party,

00:42:44.660 --> 00:42:45.660
only a month old,

00:42:46.080 --> 00:42:47.020
challenges the

00:42:48.381 --> 00:42:51.962
180-year-old Conservative party in the EU election campaign.

00:42:52.082 --> 00:42:52.342
You know,

00:42:52.382 --> 00:42:52.702
it's funny,

00:42:52.722 --> 00:42:53.122
isn't it?

00:42:53.522 --> 00:42:53.982
It worked.

00:42:54.823 --> 00:42:57.603
The Brexit party wins almost 32%

00:42:58.144 --> 00:42:58.744
of the vote.

00:42:59.384 --> 00:42:59.564
Oh

00:43:00.012 --> 00:43:04.134
All the establishment both here in the UK and over there were aghast,

00:43:04.774 --> 00:43:06.215
utterly aghast,

00:43:06.675 --> 00:43:09.056
because the penny then dropped.

00:43:10.316 --> 00:43:15.158
The Brexit Party Limited enters the EU Parliament in Brussels.

00:43:15.859 --> 00:43:16.419
Their goal?

00:43:16.959 --> 00:43:21.501
To disrupt the sessions as a final insult to the hated institution.

00:43:26.423 --> 00:43:26.663
You know,

00:43:26.703 --> 00:43:27.443
I'm a businessman.

00:43:27.724 --> 00:43:28.604
I'm a real estate guy.

00:43:29.537 --> 00:43:33.223
I'm not an experienced politician and suddenly all of a sudden we were the biggest

00:43:33.864 --> 00:43:39.414
Single political party in the whole of the European Parliament and my goodness we made a noise

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:44.802
There's an historic battle going on now across the West in Europe,

00:43:45.162 --> 00:43:46.563
America and elsewhere.

00:43:47.244 --> 00:43:50.766
It is globalism against populism.

00:43:51.446 --> 00:43:52.867
And you may loathe populism,

00:43:53.567 --> 00:43:54.648
but I'll tell you a funny thing,

00:43:55.148 --> 00:43:56.409
it's becoming very popular.

00:43:58.991 --> 00:44:00.251
I know you're going to miss us,

00:44:00.852 --> 00:44:02.633
but we're going to wave you goodbye.

00:44:03.573 --> 00:44:07.736
And we'll look forward in the future to working with you as sovereign...

00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:11.381
If you disobey the rules,

00:44:11.462 --> 00:44:12.322
you get cut off.

00:44:13.143 --> 00:44:14.805
Could we please remove the flags?

00:44:16.746 --> 00:44:20.930
But the Brexit Party is not concerned with the EU Parliament in Brussels.

00:44:21.511 --> 00:44:24.133
It is trying to exert influence in London,

00:44:24.794 --> 00:44:25.754
and it's succeeding.

00:44:29.398 --> 00:44:35.083
Large numbers of Conservative voters went to support the Brexit Party.

00:44:36.404 --> 00:44:41.025
And I think there was a sense of panic within the Conservative Party,

00:44:41.445 --> 00:44:43.886
reaching up to members of the Cabinet,

00:44:44.406 --> 00:44:46.427
that unless the Conservative Party,

00:44:47.207 --> 00:44:47.627
if you like,

00:44:47.847 --> 00:44:50.008
closed off this issue,

00:44:50.488 --> 00:44:54.089
unless it dealt with the threat of Nigel Farage,

00:44:55.989 --> 00:44:57.410
the Conservative Party was doomed.

00:44:57.510 --> 00:44:59.130
We were essentially like special forces.

00:44:59.530 --> 00:45:05.212
We launched and we came out of base and we basically accomplished the mission.

00:45:05.552 --> 00:45:06.092
We were being...

00:45:06.172 --> 00:45:10.579
betrayed by the elitist class and we stopped it we stopped the great betrayal

00:45:11.420 --> 00:45:15.562
The follow-on effect was that the day after the European elections,

00:45:15.882 --> 00:45:17.543
Theresa May knew that she was toast,

00:45:18.123 --> 00:45:19.644
and she announced that she was going to step down.

00:45:20.044 --> 00:45:23.946
Never forget that compromise is not a dirty word.

00:45:24.646 --> 00:45:27.208
Life depends on compromise.

00:45:28.668 --> 00:45:31.530
Theresa May's time as Prime Minister is over,

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:32.630
and with it,

00:45:32.990 --> 00:45:35.572
the idea of a soft Brexit compromise.

00:45:37.900 --> 00:45:47.128
There was a belief that there was only one person who would be able to stop Nigel Farage from essentially overtaking the Conservative Party,

00:45:47.429 --> 00:45:48.550
and that was Boris Johnson.

00:46:03.569 --> 00:46:09.253
People were so sick after three years of this kind of gridlock under May.

00:46:09.333 --> 00:46:10.694
They just wanted it done.

00:46:10.754 --> 00:46:13.215
Get Brexit done was a very powerful slogan.

00:46:13.495 --> 00:46:13.896
In a way,

00:46:13.996 --> 00:46:17.598
what they were doing was giving power to simplicity.

00:46:17.658 --> 00:46:19.739
It had been about take back control.

00:46:20.220 --> 00:46:20.820
We will cut...

00:46:20.900 --> 00:46:23.885
the ties to this evil empire and sail off into the sunset.

00:46:23.905 --> 00:46:25.167
It was that kind of imagery.

00:46:26.870 --> 00:46:33.461
Many moderate conservatives have to accept that their party has changed significantly in the Brexit process.

00:46:35.084 --> 00:46:35.504
And so,

00:46:36.044 --> 00:46:36.905
to a large extent,

00:46:36.965 --> 00:46:39.446
because of fear of Nigel Farage,

00:46:40.106 --> 00:46:47.189
the Conservative Party moved to become much more like Nigel Farage by being a more populist party.

00:46:47.589 --> 00:46:47.709
So

00:46:48.449 --> 00:46:52.831
I made it clear that I would resign from government because I didn't want to play any part in that.

00:46:56.884 --> 00:46:58.265
When the new Prime Minister,

00:46:58.645 --> 00:46:59.485
Boris Johnson,

00:46:59.585 --> 00:47:00.625
presents his cabinet,

00:47:00.986 --> 00:47:06.127
it is full of heartliners who are uncompromisingly for a hard Brexit.

00:47:09.469 --> 00:47:12.090
The eventual victory of Boris Johnson,

00:47:12.290 --> 00:47:14.570
his election as Conservative leader and Prime Minister,

00:47:14.911 --> 00:47:19.432
was really the ultimate victory for the money men of British politics,

00:47:19.433 --> 00:47:22.473
the kind of people who'd bankrolled the Brexit referendum,

00:47:22.673 --> 00:47:24.374
the kind of people who'd funded...

00:47:24.534 --> 00:47:26.394
pro-Brexit campaigns after Brexit,

00:47:26.675 --> 00:47:29.815
the kind of people who give money to anonymously funded think tanks,

00:47:30.075 --> 00:47:30.716
for all of them,

00:47:31.036 --> 00:47:34.957
Boris Johnson was the epitome of what they wanted.

00:47:35.097 --> 00:47:36.057
They believe he's malleable,

00:47:36.257 --> 00:47:37.337
so they believe they can push him.

00:47:38.377 --> 00:47:38.517
Well,

00:47:38.577 --> 00:47:38.978
good morning,

00:47:38.998 --> 00:47:39.398
everybody,

00:47:39.718 --> 00:47:43.699
and it is wonderful to see this new team assembled here.

00:47:43.700 --> 00:47:46.259
Thank you all very much.

00:47:46.319 --> 00:47:46.940
Cheers

00:47:49.040 --> 00:47:55.702
Our research reveals that Johnson again attracts record donations for his candidacy for party leader.

00:47:56.482 --> 00:48:02.144
He wins back to the Conservatives some of the Brexit Party's most generous financial supporters.

00:48:02.765 --> 00:48:03.225
Once again,

00:48:03.545 --> 00:48:05.846
he receives money from Anthony Bamford,

00:48:06.226 --> 00:48:08.967
the owner of JCB and CTF Partners,

00:48:09.467 --> 00:48:12.328
the firm that has supported him in previous campaigns.

00:48:14.140 --> 00:48:16.061
Largely unnoticed by the public,

00:48:16.501 --> 00:48:26.104
more than a dozen former employees of the Institute of Economic Affairs and other hard Brexit think tanks are brought into the government as advisers.

00:48:27.924 --> 00:48:28.564
Statement,

00:48:29.185 --> 00:48:29.985
the Prime Minister.

00:48:34.566 --> 00:48:39.848
To get...

00:48:40.356 --> 00:48:42.979
Exit done and let's take this country forward.

00:48:46.523 --> 00:48:50.027
On January the 31st 2020,

00:48:50.548 --> 00:48:52.610
Britain formally leaves the EU.

00:48:54.853 --> 00:48:55.594
Without Nigel,

00:48:55.595 --> 00:48:56.295
as we all know,

00:48:56.675 --> 00:48:58.257
there would have been no referendum.

00:48:59.218 --> 00:49:00.179
And without Nigel...

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:02.241
there would have been no Brexit.

00:49:02.801 --> 00:49:05.543
We become a democratic,

00:49:06.003 --> 00:49:06.783
self-governing,

00:49:07.203 --> 00:49:07.984
independent,

00:49:08.364 --> 00:49:10.745
and I hope proud nation.

00:49:12.646 --> 00:49:13.207
Five,

00:49:13.567 --> 00:49:14.027
four,

00:49:14.467 --> 00:49:14.868
three,

00:49:15.348 --> 00:49:15.468
two,

00:49:16.348 --> 00:49:20.411
one.

00:49:22.372 --> 00:49:22.492
We.

00:49:27.200 --> 00:49:27.800
During an

00:49:28.501 --> 00:49:29.701
11-month transition phase,

00:49:29.982 --> 00:49:32.943
in which Britain stays closely aligned with the EU,

00:49:33.443 --> 00:49:37.666
Johnson's government negotiates the concrete details of a trade deal.

00:49:38.626 --> 00:49:40.407
In the midst of the Covid crisis,

00:49:40.687 --> 00:49:43.969
an agreement is reached literally at the last minute.

00:49:48.192 --> 00:49:50.113
The deal brings relief in Brussels,

00:49:50.473 --> 00:49:52.674
even though it's an uneasy compromise.

00:49:53.554 --> 00:49:55.515
Mission accomplished.

00:49:56.516 --> 00:49:58.860
Part Brexit has become reality.

00:50:00.162 --> 00:50:01.244
On December 30,

00:50:01.245 --> 00:50:02.145
2020,

00:50:02.486 --> 00:50:04.589
Boris Johnson signs the agreement.

00:50:06.352 --> 00:50:06.652
Folks,

00:50:06.672 --> 00:50:07.153
this is it.

00:50:08.115 --> 00:50:08.375
And

00:50:08.756 --> 00:50:10.859
I know the question you will all be asking yourselves is...

00:50:11.760 --> 00:50:12.220
Have I rigged?

00:50:12.221 --> 00:50:13.941
The answer is yes,

00:50:14.501 --> 00:50:16.841
and it's an excellent deal for this country.

00:50:17.362 --> 00:50:18.262
Thank you all very much.

00:50:18.502 --> 00:50:19.042
Thank you.

00:50:19.102 --> 00:50:21.583
I do think it's a huge mistake for the country.

00:50:21.743 --> 00:50:24.844
I think others are going to pay a very significant price for that,

00:50:24.884 --> 00:50:27.164
those who are going to lose their jobs as a consequence of it.

00:50:27.384 --> 00:50:31.966
I think the UK is a less attractive place for inward investment.

00:50:32.806 --> 00:50:35.426
And I think there are many ordinary British people,

00:50:35.827 --> 00:50:41.188
probably ordinary British people who voted to leave in 2016 and voted Conservative in 2019.

00:50:42.408 --> 00:50:44.889
who will sadly pay a price for this.

00:50:45.509 --> 00:50:48.891
Of course there's no such thing as just getting Brexit done.

00:50:48.951 --> 00:50:49.491
Of course

00:50:49.851 --> 00:50:54.692
Brexit and its complications are going to dominate the British political debate for the next ten years.

00:50:55.513 --> 00:50:56.693
That's just the way it is.

00:50:56.993 --> 00:50:57.633
At the end of the day,

00:50:57.693 --> 00:50:58.934
there's still this very big question mark.

00:50:59.094 --> 00:51:00.154
What is it going to achieve,

00:51:00.194 --> 00:51:04.496
apart from a lot of economic pain for the UK and a lot of hardship and poverty?

00:51:05.640 --> 00:51:07.962
How will the UK use its new freedom?

00:51:08.702 --> 00:51:10.603
Just one week after the final exit,

00:51:10.884 --> 00:51:16.587
hardliners have already called for the abolition of environmental data protection and labour laws.

00:51:17.248 --> 00:51:19.589
The battle over Brexit will continue.

00:51:20.490 --> 00:51:26.493
the coming years will show how much this coup by the country's rich elite will change Great Britain.

00:51:26.713 --> 00:51:28.454
I think too often we don't appreciate,

00:51:28.455 --> 00:51:32.095
we don't understand how abuse of the electoral system,

00:51:32.235 --> 00:51:40.619
an abusive political system and the role of lobbying unchecked in politics can change and shift and kind of warp a political landscape.

